# Every tracked text file in this repository is stored LF. Without this file a
# Windows checkout converts all of them - 632 of 638 were `i/lf w/crlf` when this
# was written, and `.gitmodules` had drifted to mixed endings in the working tree.
#
# Nothing is broken by that today, and the reason is worth recording so it is not
# mistaken for the problem being absent: `scripts/update-solutions.ps1` normalises
# with .Replace("`r`n", "`n") before it compares, so the Solution manifest check
# defends itself. That is one tool remembering to. The component this repository
# composes learned the same lesson the other way - a stock Windows clone of
# Broiler.VM failed five tests before any edit, because three generated artefacts
# and an eighty-seven-entry SHA-256-pinned corpus are compared byte for byte and
# nothing normalised for them.
#
# Pinning the checkout is cheaper than requiring every future byte-comparing tool
# to remember. Submodules carry their own .gitattributes; this file governs only
# what this repository tracks.
#
# This changes no content. All 638 tracked text files are already LF in the index,
# so `git add --renormalize .` reports nothing but this file.

# Text is stored and checked out LF on every platform, whatever a contributor's
# core.autocrlf says. eol=lf is the half that matters: text=auto alone normalises
# the index and still hands a Windows checkout CRLF.
*             text=auto eol=lf

*.cs          text eol=lf
*.csproj      text eol=lf
*.slnx        text eol=lf
*.props       text eol=lf
*.targets     text eol=lf
*.pubxml      text eol=lf
*.xml         text eol=lf
*.js          text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.ps1         text eol=lf
*.config      text eol=lf
.gitmodules   text eol=lf

*.png         binary
